INSERT INTO episode (slug, number, title, blurb, style, audio_key, transcript_key, duration_s, session_number) VALUES ('the-master-and-the-disciple', 1, 'Three Thanks and the Persian Awakening', 'Episode one of *The Master and the Disciple* opens on a group of the truly faithful and a group of du''at al-din standing in front of one of their scholars with three thanks owed at once — for the call, for the knowledge, for the practice — and asking how on earth they are supposed to repay them. The Master answers first with a metaphysics of stages: every beginning has been given an end, every end a goal, every goal a limit whose full extent cannot be reached. He names the three-stage spiritual path balwa-huda-taqwa, and only then names the three deeds — obedience for the call, action for the knowledge, perseverance for the practice. The episode then enters the story of the Persian seeker, the man whose thirst for knowledge led him through mirage after mirage until he reached the place of nothing and met Allah there. From that meeting he came under the same proverb that had once been his master''s: ''The most excellent of deeds is giving life to the dead.'' His interior monologue runs the chain backward — did the trust really descend from the heavenly host through every intermediary only to stop with him? The answer requires action. So he sets out across the Arab and non-Arab lands, and the episode lands with him at the far edge of al-jazira, the disciple still unfound.
INSERT INTO episode (slug, number, title, blurb, style, audio_key, transcript_key, duration_s, session_number) VALUES ('the-master-and-the-disciple', 2, 'The Test of Speech and the Design of Creation', 'Episode two of *The Master and the Disciple* picks up the Persian scholar at the far edge of al-jazira. He enters a city where nobody knows him, sits out of sight to listen to a group of townspeople debate religion without a guide, and picks them — for three precise reasons — as deserving of life: the hujja has reached them; they belong to the milla; and they are already inquiring. They test his speech, and he passes the test by refusing authorship: ''I have no words of my own, for I am following the words of another.'' Recognized as a gateway, he rises and delivers the great design-of-creation sermon, the book''s central cosmology, in which the outer and the inner are named as a deliberate mechanism of preservation rather than a textual layer. The episode then turns to the instrument by which the listener uses the chain — hearing, sight, and the heart, named together in four Quranic verses as gift, as sealed, as covered, and as the very things by which Allah will hold the listener to account. The episode lands on the youngest of the group following the scholar to his inn — the disciple chosen.
INSERT INTO episode (slug, number, title, blurb, style, audio_key, transcript_key, duration_s, session_number) VALUES ('the-master-and-the-disciple', 3, 'Five Conditions and the Covenant', 'Episode three of *The Master and the Disciple* delivers the structural climax of the opening narrative arc. The young man has begged to be raised; the scholar answers with the infant analogy and the graduated ladder of spiritual learning. Before he agrees to teach a single doctrine, he sets the precondition: knowledge is of no benefit until it has been put into action, and action is of no benefit until it is accepted, and the only thing Allah accepts is sincerity. He then imposes five conditions — and the fifth reveals the political stake: the boy''s father is a known leader of a rival community that has rejected the Imam, and premature disclosure of the da''wa could destroy the mission. The conditions are protocols of taqiyya, not pedagogy. After a period of trial, the meeting is arranged. The scholar names the key of religion — the ''ahd Allah — and delivers the analogy: marriage and fornication are the same act, distinguished by the nikah; halal and haram are the same animal, distinguished by the dhabiha; sacred and stolen are the same religious practice, distinguished by walaya. Imam Ja''far al-Sadiq''s apple-and-charity teaching locks the doctrine: a stolen apple given to charity propagates the haram and earns ten sins, not forty-five rewards. The episode lands on the title-teaches move — disciple from discipline, discipline from shari''a — and on the night of the oath, the boy''s tears, his certainty of belonging to the Hizb Allah.
INSERT INTO episode (slug, number, title, blurb, style, audio_key, transcript_key, duration_s, session_number) VALUES ('the-master-and-the-disciple', 4, 'Originator and the Three Primordial Words', 'Episode four of *The Master and the Disciple* begins the doctrinal core of the book with the architecture of creation laid down with mathematical precision. The scholar starts with what kind of First Allah is — Originator without template, first of every first, inheritor of all existence. He names the three primordial words that fall from the first Light: will, command, speech — densifying as they descend. From those three words come seven principles: air the unpaired first and king, then water, darkness, and physical light, then their denser pairs of smoke, mud, and fire. From smoke come the seven heavens; from mud the seven earths. And the letters give the numerical signature: seven in the command kun fa-yakun, twelve in the three primordial words — stamped into the cosmos as seven heavens, seven oceans, seven days; twelve zodiacs, twelve regions, twelve hours of day and twelve of night. The signature of cosmology turns out to be the letter-count of its own genesis.
INSERT INTO episode (slug, number, title, blurb, style, audio_key, transcript_key, duration_s, session_number) VALUES ('the-master-and-the-disciple', 5, 'Bismillah Seal and the Chosen Ranks', 'Episode five of *The Master and the Disciple* shows how the architecture of creation is sealed into the Bismillah — a cryptographic lock whose three names and letter-counts mirror the three primordial words and the sevens-and-twelves of cosmology, point for point. Then it names who fills the houses of prophethood: distinguished personalities and guiding figures by category and function — Natiq, Imam, Bab, Hujaj, Nuqaba, Du''at, ulama. And it mirrors the hierarchy in the visible sky: the Imam is the sun, his Bab the moon, the Du''at the stars. A finer distinction is preserved that listeners often miss: the twelve regional Hujaj are proofs, but the Bab is the one Gateway — the Hujja al-Imam, the single moon to the one sun.
INSERT INTO episode (slug, number, title, blurb, style, audio_key, transcript_key, duration_s, session_number) VALUES ('the-master-and-the-disciple', 6, 'Zahir, Batin, and the Seven Natiqs', 'Episode six of *The Master and the Disciple* names the seven speaking prophets — Adam, Noah, Abraham, Moses, Jesus, Muhammad, Mahdi — and shows how the calendar of cosmology (sevens, twelves, thirties) is the calendar of spiritual transmission. Then it gives the deepest framing of the whole architecture: zahir and batin are not two textual layers but the body and the spirit of religion, each subsisting only through the other. Religion is the soul of the revealed paths; the ordinances of shari''a are the body. Each lives through the other; neither lives without the other. Finally the scholar answers the disciple''s question about the world by walking the visible cosmos as a catalogue of symbols: the heaven points to the Natiq; the zodiac to the Nuqaba; the earth to the Bab and Wasi; the islands to the Hujaj; the rivers and springs to the Du''at. And the salty sea encodes outward knowledge while the sweet spring encodes inner knowledge — both are water, both are life, but only one feeds the field.
INSERT INTO episode (slug, number, title, blurb, style, audio_key, transcript_key, duration_s, session_number) VALUES ('the-master-and-the-disciple', 7, 'Air and the Instance Beyond Air', 'Episode seven of *The Master and the Disciple* takes the architecture of creation to its lofty edge. One element of the cosmology has been waiting since the very first concept: Air — the first of the seven, the unpaired king, what mixes with everything and is encompassed by nothing. The disciple asks about it. The scholar refuses to make air a symbol of any creation, then derives — through three nested scale comparisons (a seal-ring on a plain, a seal-ring in a desert, an egg on a plain) — why air points only upward. The disciple raises a Quranic objection that seems to topple the whole teaching: do not strike likenesses for Allah. And the scholar turns it into the doctrine''s strongest pillar with a second verse from the same chapter: and Allah''s is the loftiest similitude. Both verses are true. Lesser signs are forbidden as peers in a series; the loftiest similitude is licensed because it is the sign that announces the failure of signs. Then the disciple pushes one rung further, and the scholar names what stands behind even Air: al-Awan, the Instance of His divine will, the doorway of irada itself. The disciple pleads to be brought back from the lofty edge. The scholar invokes Moses and the scholar — Khidr — and closes the dialogue at its lofty edge, leaving the inner of the inner unopened.
INSERT INTO episode (slug, number, title, blurb, style, audio_key, transcript_key, duration_s, session_number) VALUES ('the-master-and-the-disciple', 8, 'Three Levels of Knowledge — Angels, Humans, and Beasts', 'Episode eight of *The Master and the Disciple* opens Part Three by turning the architecture inside out. The disciple asks whether the inner has an inner, and the scholar answers yes — there is an outer, an inner, and an innermost of that inner aspect, and the triple structure is everywhere in Allah''s book and in the very grammar of how anything is understood. The verse of the garment carries it. The grammar of name, attribute, and meaning carries it. The shell, white, and yolk of an egg carries it. And then the scholar maps the three layers of knowledge onto three levels of creatures: the beasts, who know only the outer; the humans, who know the inner; and the angels, who know the inner of the inner — at whose rung sit the prophets, Allah''s vicegerents on earth. The disciple asks the question that follows: can the highest rung be climbed from below? The scholar answers with the farmer who cannot make the plant grow and with the rope of Allah held tightly through the Imam — reached through his proofs and his gateway. The disciple completes the move himself: there is no power and no strength except through Allah. The next episode will open that formula and show what has been waiting inside it.
INSERT INTO episode (slug, number, title, blurb, style, audio_key, transcript_key, duration_s, session_number) VALUES ('the-master-and-the-disciple', 9, 'Hawl and Quwwa, and the Dream of Joseph', 'Episode nine of *The Master and the Disciple* takes apart the line every Muslim recites in difficulty: there is no power and no strength except through Allah. The disciple offers the common readings — hawl as "a year," quwwa as "mules" — and the scholar walks them into absurdity before opening the inner meaning. Hawl is the Imam who converts the Creator''s speech into the words of wisdom that human beings can hear; quwwa is the Bab who bears the divine speech in its condensed, all-inclusive form and distributes it to the Du''at in the form each can carry. The formula is the confession of an entire chain that is itself dependent at every link. Then the same architecture is read out of two of the great dreams of Surat Yusuf — Joseph''s eleven stars and the sun and the moon (chapter 12, verse 4) and the king''s seven fat cows and seven green ears — and the cosmology of Part Two is heard inside Part Three''s formulae and dreams.
INSERT INTO episode (slug, number, title, blurb, style, audio_key, transcript_key, duration_s, session_number) VALUES ('the-master-and-the-disciple', 10, 'The Union of Inner and Outer', 'Episode ten of *The Master and the Disciple* answers the question every reader of esoteric doctrine must ask. If the inner is everything, what becomes of the outer — of prayer, of fasting, of the daily obligations of shari''a? The scholar answers in three movements. First the union of inner and outer as a single living religion: the seeker who keeps only the inner is fruit rotting before ripening; the one who keeps only the outer is a body without spirit, numbered among the dead; only their union is faith, and for the believer this world becomes licit. Second the pale faces of the true scholars: not a refusal of the world but the body''s record of vigilant attention to the next, distracted from luxury by reflection and engrossed in the sweetness of Allah''s remembrance. And third the doctrine of divine justice in acceptance: the instrument of knowledge is identical in every chest; the difference is in what each seeker does with it. The episode closes with mercy under necessity, told through the thirsting woman in the time of the second caliph and the Father of Imams'' ruling that the verse of necessity left her free of sin.
INSERT INTO episode (slug, number, title, blurb, style, audio_key, transcript_key, duration_s, session_number) VALUES ('the-master-and-the-disciple', 11, 'Purifying Possessions and Parting from the Master', 'Episode eleven of *The Master and the Disciple* closes the dialogue between scholar and disciple. The disciple''s question is the most concrete he has asked: what do I do with what I own? The scholar gives a rule — lawful acquisition; zakat from the whole; khums from the finest portion you love most; the rest for family with the surplus to the needy. The disciple surprises him by having already divided his possessions into five portions with pedagogical weights — zakat; atonement kept as remembrance of sins; gratitude held as a trust until maturity; brothers'' portion given by his own hand; and a portion to support the scholar. The scholar weeps and refuses his own portion, redirecting each of the others and turning the purification portion into a trust for the Imam — named here as the rightful judge whose hands hold the keys of the gardens and whose palms reveal the light of Sinai. Then the scholar travels to consult the Sheikh, his own spiritual father, who instructs him to hurry back because the young man is to become a gateway of loving kindness in his own time. And when the scholar returns he finds the disciple pale and weak with longing — ishq, the source names it, extreme love at separation from the beloved. The scholar announces the journey, and the disciple sighs so deeply with joy and awe that his spirit almost leaves his body.
INSERT INTO episode (slug, number, title, blurb, style, audio_key, transcript_key, duration_s, session_number) VALUES ('the-master-and-the-disciple', 12, 'Rebirth of the Disciple and the Seven-Day Tawaf', 'Episode twelve of *The Master and the Disciple* opens Part Four. The disciple and the scholar reach the Sheikh. The Sheikh begins with a sustained praise of Allah — justice as the balance between command and prohibition, the honor of intellect as its use in the search, the duties of truth as obligatory, the signs to be shown in the cosmos and in the self. Then he turns to the disciple and refuses every name offered until the disciple has no answer left. A name, he says, can only be given after seven days, in honor of the new-born infant; the universe itself received its life from the seventh letter, and the embryo passes through seven stages before another creation is brought forth. On the seventh day, after ablution and a fresh garment, the disciple is drawn close, taught the realities pens cannot write, and brought into a tawaf the source reads as the planets orbiting their sun — the Imam as the centre, gravity as love, marifa as the orbit in which the planet''s life is held.
INSERT INTO episode (slug, number, title, blurb, style, audio_key, transcript_key, duration_s, session_number) VALUES ('the-master-and-the-disciple', 13, 'Homecoming and the Forty-Year Syllogism', 'Episode thirteen of *The Master and the Disciple* sends the disciple home. The Sheikh releases him for three reasons — his own travel, the father''s sorrow, the disciple''s new obligation to transmit — and binds him with four counsels: be mindful of Allah, uphold the oath, safeguard the scholar at his side, keep his identity concealed. At the outskirts of the city the scholar names his residence and gives six parting counsels that read as a da''i''s operating manual. Resolution entrusted to Allah; words of wisdom in calling; no anger at being wronged; no upset at being called a liar; patience with the responder; being the best example. The disciple thanks him for three things — the Imam whose argument was upheld, the hujja whose trust was kept, the corpse to which life was given — kisses his feet, and goes home to a father whose charge has two horns: you concealed the truth from me, or you destroyed yourself by deception. The disciple meets the syllogism with a syllogism. Over forty years either you were a scholar who withheld, or you were ignorant who should have preceded me. Every door closes. The father weeps and enters the path; through father and son a great number of people are revived.
INSERT INTO episode (slug, number, title, blurb, style, audio_key, transcript_key, duration_s, session_number) VALUES ('the-master-and-the-disciple', 14, 'Abu Malik Arrives and the Fair Inquiry', 'Episode fourteen of *The Master and the Disciple* brings the champion of the opposing community on stage. The former co-religionists of al-Bakhtari, who are afraid of losing his favors and afraid of being on the wrong side of his conversion, send for the Yemeni scholar Abu Malik — Kab al-Ahbar of his age, the glory of the yearned. He responds with striking fairness. He refuses to condemn the two converts without hearing them and refuses to assume their guidance without testing it; both, he says, would be injustice. He warns his own companions against the satisfaction of past communities who said no messenger would come after theirs and did not see the way of proceeding beyond what they had attained. He gives the conversion three possible readings — decorative addition; real increase of knowledge as the believer''s lost camel; or true prophetic guidance — under each of which the right move is to go and meet. He names the inversion of signs by which falsehood wears conformity and truth wears trial, and defines the sound heart as the one that does not deny what enters it. He insists on humility before the truth, not after. And he refuses delay: today, not in three days, not in two. The greatest scholar of the opposing camp has just set the terms of his own debate by the standard of justice.
INSERT INTO episode (slug, number, title, blurb, style, audio_key, transcript_key, duration_s, session_number) VALUES ('the-master-and-the-disciple', 15, 'Emptying the Cup — The Demolisher''s Art', 'Episode fifteen of *The Master and the Disciple* opens the central debate of Part Four. Abu Malik arrives at al-Bakhtari''s door and is met by a threefold riddle that re-arranges every hierarchy he carries with him — the son is the father; the Lord is on earth with a servant; the mortal lives forever. Abu Malik mistakes it for news of death and recites the verse for the deceased. Inside the house, Salih receives him with perfect manners and prays for the ears of his heart to be opened. Then Salih asks which of three kinds of seeker he is — the one who knows, the one beginning to know, or the one who desires to know. Abu Malik admits he does not know the modes of seeking at all and places himself among the beginners. The source steps back and names the technique: kasrah, demolition; the rank in the spiritual hierarchy that performs it is the mukasir, the Demolisher. Then Salih draws the distinction that will govern everything that follows: ilm versus marifa, knowledge versus gnosis, khabar versus iyan, report versus vision. The alim knows heaven and hell exist; the arif burns to reach the one and avoid the other. Abu Malik confesses he found that intense neediness in his own soul. Salih cautions him against rushing the rebuilding; every seed requires its ground to be prepared, and every knowledge requires a heart first purified by proper argument.
INSERT INTO episode (slug, number, title, blurb, style, audio_key, transcript_key, duration_s, session_number) VALUES ('the-master-and-the-disciple', 16, 'Jewels, the Moon, and the Description of Religion', 'Episode sixteen of *The Master and the Disciple* closes the great debate of Part Four. Abu Malik invites Salih to state his arguments under the image of wisdom as jewels in the sunlight; Salih accepts the image with one condition — it holds only where there is no confusion between real jewel and forged glass. The story of the jewelers'' verdict on his former faith leads to the principle that spiritual knowledge must be presented to those who receive it from the heavens before it can be deemed genuine. Abu Malik asks who, after the prophets, such people can be; Salih answers by refusing the standing of any religion grounded only in transmitted reports. The example is moon-sighting. The hadith of moon- sighting has turned, Salih says, into the hadith of moon-fighting. The Messenger''s instruction "follow the moon" was the designation of the wasi the community was to follow — the moon as the established symbol, the wasi who follows the sun''s path — and the community collapsed the symbol into the disc. The Quran''s own description of the sun and moon on fixed calculated paths witnesses against the chaos. A divorce case study — a man left not knowing whether he is married or divorced — brings Abu Malik to the confession that sharia has been corrupted. Salih dissolves the religion=sharia equivalence by which Abu Malik had been holding what was left and gives his own description of religion: the veil, the rope from the heavens, the firm handhold, the ark of the divine presence, the ship of salvation, the light of life, the well- protected knowledge, the jewels with their distinctive signs. Abu Malik accepts the description as the essential meaning religion would have to have and asks for the reality of which the description is the picture. The way is opened for the final part of the debate.
INSERT INTO episode (slug, number, title, blurb, style, audio_key, transcript_key, duration_s, session_number) VALUES ('the-master-and-the-disciple', 17, 'Who Is Allah? — Beyond Names and Adjectives', 'Episode seventeen of *The Master and the Disciple* opens the climax of the debate. Salih refuses to let the conversation about Allah''s justice and unity happen at the level of the school''s standard creed. Names cannot cook food. The four letters of the divine name are signs, not containers. The analogical proof from manufactured things secretly forges a likeness. The standard formula ''nothing like His image'' even smuggles an image back under the table. The eye that cannot bear the sun cannot weigh the Creator. By the end Abu Malik says it himself: his words have left him; intellects are bewildered; imaginations cannot reach Him. That confession is the doorway into the next episode''s syllogism.
INSERT INTO episode (slug, number, title, blurb, style, audio_key, transcript_key, duration_s, session_number) VALUES ('the-master-and-the-disciple', 18, 'The Syllogism of Divine Justice', 'Episode eighteen of *The Master and the Disciple* delivers the syllogism the whole book has been building toward. Allah cannot be sensed. Allah cannot be unjust. Therefore a chain of just witnesses must extend Allah''s command to creatures who could not receive it otherwise. Their existence is Allah''s act of justice. The chain has rank-order in function but equality in reward. The trustee carries the esoteric interpretation as a second channel of the same divine knowledge that placed the outer book. The chain cannot be cut without converting Allah''s blessing into the withdrawal of the means of worship — which divine justice forbids. Abu Malik affirms it as the very foundation of justice.
INSERT INTO episode (slug, number, title, blurb, style, audio_key, transcript_key, duration_s, session_number) VALUES ('the-master-and-the-disciple', 19, 'The Conspiracy Formula', 'Episode nineteen of *The Master and the Disciple* delivers the book''s most distinctive piece of social analysis — the conspiracy formula. Three actors in every period — a rebellious devil, a willing oppressor, and a hypocritical religious scholar — collaborate to sever the outward traces of prophecy from its living continuation so that the traces can be exploited as a commodity. The slogan that holds the severance in place is ''no prophet after our prophet,'' propagated until inquiry itself becomes redundant. Salih traces the pattern across the Zoroastrian, Torah, and Gospel communities, names this community as the latest case, and turns the consensus argument back: by that very logic, the earlier communities hold the greater claim. The Creator''s freedom to abrogate and confirm voids every closure argument anyway.
INSERT INTO episode (slug, number, title, blurb, style, audio_key, transcript_key, duration_s, session_number) VALUES ('the-master-and-the-disciple', 20, 'Unbroken Chain and the Path of Return', 'The final episode of *The Master and the Disciple*. Salih refutes the very notion of an interval between prophets by naming the chain link by link, and rereads the Quranic word fatra as a period of fear in which the call is made in secret rather than as an absence of the witness from the earth. The concealment of the friends of Allah in the listener''s own time is explained by the way of Moses fleeing into Midian. The three categories of people — relentless murderer, rageful enemy, sympathetic abandoner — locate the moral weight not on the enemies but on those who feel for the witnesses but abandon them. The scholars who legitimate the oppressors are named as worse than the murderers themselves. The path of return is brief and exact: help the friends of Allah and they will reveal themselves; abandon their enemies and they will not be victorious. Abu Malik repents. The source seals the book by declaring its arc a replicable model from individual conversion to community-building. The closing praise-and-blessings seal closes the work.
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('the-master-and-the-disciple', 'the persian who was dead and revived', 'student:cb85bbdfdc2c536d', 1, 'one of the scholars of the Mount', 'one of the scholars of the Mount', '<p>The old scholars of the family read the Quran on two levels at once — the words on the surface, and the meaning underneath. Whenever a mountain or a house is named, they ask what rank of faith it points to, because sacred geography in this reading is a map of <strong>people, not of land</strong>.</p><p>The Mount (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اَلطُّور</span>) is where Musa was called and spoke with his Lord — the high place where knowledge comes down from above.</p><p><span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَالطُّورِ</span> &quot;By the Mount.&quot; Al-Tur 52:1</p><p>To be a scholar of the Mount is not to stand on a hill in Sinai. It is to have climbed to the <strong>rank where teaching is received directly and then handed on</strong> — the place of the one who instructs others in the faith.</p><p>The Frequented House (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اَلْبَيْتُ الْمَعْمُور</span>) is the house in the heavens that is never empty — just as the Kaʿba is circled below, this house above is forever thronged by those who worship and carry the teaching.</p><p><span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَالْبَيْتِ الْمَعْمُورِ</span> &quot;By the Frequented House.&quot; Al-Tur 52:4</p><p>To dwell there is to belong to the <strong>living community of believers that is always full</strong>, always receiving and always passing on — never a locked, abandoned room.</p><p>So picture our Persian. He was dead in ignorance, then revived by knowledge, and the moment his guidance was complete two things became true of him at once:</p><ol><li><p>He joined those who teach — a scholar of the Mount.</p></li><li><p>He joined those who are taught and who keep the House full — a dweller at the Frequented House.</p></li></ol><p>He receives from above and gives below in the same breath. The one who was gained becomes the one who gains others, so that the very thanksgiving once owed <em>to</em> him becomes thanksgiving owed <em>by</em> him.</p>', '["اَلطُّور (the Mount): from the root ط-و-ر, which carries the sense of a stage, a boundary, or a limit that one crosses over — as in an atwar, a phase of growth. The Mount is the elevated threshold a seeker crosses to reach the place where he meets his Lord and receives what lies beyond.", "اَلْبَيْتُ الْمَعْمُور (the Frequented House): from the root ع-م-ر, meaning to fill with life, to inhabit, to keep in flourishing use — the same root gives umr, a lifespan, and imara, cultivation. A house that is ma''mur is kept alive by constant visitors, never deserted, which is exactly the community of believers this rank names."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('the-master-and-the-disciple', 'the persian who was dead and revived', 'student:2c7e32cf885ec751', 2, 'These are the houses of the people of understanding, and their keys a…', 'These are the houses of the people of understanding, and their keys are remembrance', '<p>When the Master speaks of &quot;houses,&quot; picture a home a seeker walks through room by room. Each house is a <strong>stage of the heart''s journey</strong> toward knowledge, and the people of understanding are those who keep walking rather than stopping at the door.</p><p>The Master names three rooms, in the order the soul actually enters them:</p><ol><li><p>Affliction (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بلاء</span>) — the ache of noticing what we lack. The Persian man was struck by the affliction of ignorance, and it was that very pain, like thirst in the desert, that first set him moving.</p></li><li><p>Guidance (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">هدى</span>) — the way shown to us once we have begun to seek. It is not earned by cleverness; it is given, the way water is given to the thirsty.</p></li><li><p>Piety (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تقوى</span>) — the guarding of what we have received, living carefully so the gift is never lost.</p></li></ol><p>The order itself is the lesson. <strong>Comfort teaches nothing</strong> — it is affliction that cracks us open and makes us ask. Guidance can only reach a heart that has already felt its own emptiness, and piety keeps that guidance alive long after the first light: first the wound, then the healing, then the tending of the scar.</p><p>And the key to every door is remembrance (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذكر</span>). A house does us no good while its door stays shut, and what opens each of these is keeping Allah in mind — in our affliction so the pain becomes a summons, in our guidance so we never forget Who gave it, and in our piety so obedience stays warm and alive instead of hardening into mere habit.</p>', '["بلاء (affliction): from the root ب-ل-و, meaning to test something or to wear it out through use. A garment worn thin is بالٍ, and a soul is likewise worn and proven by trial — so بلاء is the testing that both wears a person down and reveals what they are truly made of.", "هدى (guidance): from the root ه-د-ي, to lead gently and to give a gift — the same root behind هدية, a present. Guidance is therefore not a demand but a gift: someone taking us by the hand toward what we could never have found on our own.", "تقوى (piety): from the root و-ق-ي, to shield or guard against harm. تقوى is not fear for its own sake but the careful shielding of the soul — placing a guard between ourselves and whatever would ruin the gift we were given.", "ذكر (remembrance): from the root ذ-ك-ر, to recall inwardly and to mention aloud. Remembrance joins the two — holding Allah in the heart while His name reaches the tongue — so that memory is never silent but keeps the seeker awake at every threshold."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('the-master-and-the-disciple', 'a stranger in the city', 'student:57d817b85486ffc8', 1, 'and its secrets are guarded by those who are their rightful keepers', 'and its secrets are guarded by those who are their rightful keepers', '<p>Think of a single lamp passed from hand to hand through the dark — that is what &quot;it&quot; is throughout this passage. The many names given to it — hidden knowledge, a deposit in the breasts of the righteous, the rule of the Judge, a thing with an outward and an inward — are not many things but <strong>one thing seen from many sides</strong>: the divine Knowledge (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">علم</span>), the living Word (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كلمة</span>) whose origin is from Allah and which calls back to Allah.</p><p>Notice how the young man refuses to claim any speech as his own; he is only a follower of the right word. So when he tells of what Allah honored, protected, and concealed, he speaks of that same Word — the guidance placed inside creation and then <strong>guarded</strong>, so that clever schemers and proud thinkers cannot seize it by the force of their intellect.</p><p>This is why it wears two faces:</p><ul><li><p>Its outward (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ظاهر</span>) is the plain remembrance and proof — the verses anyone can hear and recite.</p></li><li><p>Its inward (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">باطن</span>) is &quot;a sure drink,&quot; the deeper meaning that quenches only those who journey toward Allah.</p></li></ul><p>The very same revelation is at once an open book for all and a hidden treasure for the few who truly seek it.</p><p>So who are its &quot;rightful keepers&quot;? Not the crowd, and not the merely lettered. They are <strong>the righteous whom Allah trusts</strong> — &quot;a trust (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أمانة</span>) which they discharge and a bound which they do not exceed.&quot; In our Ismaili understanding these are the Friends of Allah (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أولياء الله</span>), the Imams and those they entrust, in whose breasts the Word is sheltered like light kept safe within a lamp. They do not invent it and they do not overstep it; they carry it faithfully and pass it onward.</p><p>And here is the tender point the passage leaves with us: this knowledge is guarded not to shut us out, but to draw us in. Seek knowledge, and we will come to know. The door is kept closed only so that we will learn to knock.</p>', '["علم (knowledge): from the root ع-ل-م, to mark or leave a sign. A landmark (عَلَم) is what guides a traveler across open land, so knowledge is the inner sign that lets the mind find its way to what is true.", "ظاهر (outward): from the root ظ-ه-ر, the back or a hill''s high ridge — what stands out and is plainly seen. It came to mean the surface of a thing, its open face on view to everyone.", "باطن (inward): from the root ب-ط-ن, the belly or the interior of a thing. From the hidden inside of the body the word grew to mean any concealed depth lying beneath the visible surface.", "أمانة (trust): from the root أ-م-ن, to be safe and secure. A trust is what you place into safe hands; the very same root gives faith (إيمان), the act of entrusting one''s whole heart.", "ولي (friend and guardian): from the root و-ل-ي, to be near with nothing in between. A walī is one drawn so close to Allah that no gap remains — at once a protector and a devoted, loving friend."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('the-master-and-the-disciple', 'a stranger in the city', 'student:ac35697c517e9b71', 2, 'by ignorance and by want, He compelled minds to seek knowledge and ca…', 'by ignorance and by want, He compelled minds to seek knowledge and capacity', '<p>Allah did not make the mind full and finished. He made it empty on purpose, and that emptiness is not a flaw but an engine. A lack is a kind of pull: the very thing we do not have is what draws us toward it. <strong>To be given a need is already to be pointed at its answer.</strong></p><p>Think of hunger. We come to the table not because we are full but because we are empty, and the emptiness is what moves our feet. Thirst is the same — the dryness drives us to water. The chapter leans on exactly this picture when it speaks of watering places for those who journey and a sure drink for those who seek. The seeking is not something added onto the thirst; the thirst <em>is</em> the seeking.</p><p>Two lacks are named here, and each drives its own search:</p><ul><li><p>ignorance (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">جهل</span>) — not knowing pushes the mind toward knowledge (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">علم</span>),</p></li><li><p>want (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فقر</span>) — not having pushes the soul toward capacity and completion.</p></li></ul><p>So when the passage says Allah compelled minds by ignorance and by want, the compulsion is gentle, not violent. He did not shout an order to seek from the outside; He built the seeking into us by leaving us short. <strong>The hole in us is the map to what fills it.</strong></p><p>That is why the chapter''s opening line carries a quiet promise — whoever seeks a thing is on the verge of finding it. To feel our own lack is not to have been abandoned. It is to have been aimed, lovingly, at the One who alone completes us.</p>', '["جهل (ignorance): from the root ج-ه-ل, to be ignorant. The same root also carries the sense of rashness and acting hastily, because one who does not know still rushes to act — so the word names not only the empty space of not-knowing but the restless push out of it toward علم.", "فقر (want): from the root ف-ق-ر, tied to فَقَار, the bones of the spine. Poverty is felt as a break in the backbone; the needy one is bent by his lack. The word carries the picture of someone bowed low, leaning toward whatever will support and straighten him again.", "علم (knowledge): from the root ع-ل-م, to know, kin to عَلامة, a mark or sign. To know is to read something by its marks. The chapter speaks of signs that point to Allah, so this knowing is exactly that — recognizing Him through the signs He set for those who look."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('the-master-and-the-disciple', 'the boy at the door — limits and conditions', 'student:cbd4af2385d91b42', 1, 'It is a part of that blessing—like a handful of water drawn from the…', 'It is a part of that blessing—like a handful of water drawn from the vast Euphrates.', '<p>Think of what the boy is offered as a single handful of water lifted from the Euphrates. The water in his palm is real river-water, nothing less — yet the river itself keeps flowing, wide and deep, long after his hand is full. So the teaching he is called to is genuinely part of the blessing (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نعمة</span>), but the blessing in its whole reality is the <strong>living spring itself</strong>: the light and inner knowledge (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">معرفة</span>) that Allah placed with His chosen friends and never lets run dry.</p><p>What the boy first receives is the covenant (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ميثاق</span>) — the doorway, the outward form of symbols and practices, the key that parts the lawful from the unlawful. But a key is not the treasure house it opens. The <strong>covenant admits him</strong>; it does not yet hand him everything the house contains.</p><p>Beyond his handful lies the inward reality the Master named: knowledge whose governing principle is light (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نور</span>), reaching finally to the guardianship (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ولاية</span>) of the one entrusted with the truth — Maulana Ali and the line after him. This deeper share is not withheld out of stinginess. Allah grants each seeker the rank that matches his readiness, so <strong>the measure grows as the one who carries it grows</strong>.</p><p>This is why he is given a part and not the whole at once. Simpler knowledge must settle in the heart before deeper knowledge can rest upon it, just as a child must be small before he can be great. Yet the river loses nothing by our drinking: the more sincerely we return to it, the more we are given — and the handful in our palm today is the promise of the whole Euphrates tomorrow.</p>', '["نعمة (blessing): from the root ن-ع-م, carrying the sense of softness, ease, and well-being — the same root behind a life made gentle and comfortable. A نعمة is thus not a cold gift but a favour that softens and nourishes the one who receives it, which is why divine grace is named by it.", "ميثاق (covenant): from the root و-ث-ق, meaning to be firm, tied fast, trustworthy. From it come a binding knot and a sure trust. A ميثاق is a bond tied so firmly it cannot slip — fitting for a pledge that binds the soul to Allah and to His friends.", "معرفة (knowledge): from the root ع-ر-ف, to recognise, to know intimately by acquaintance. Unlike bare information, معرفة is the knowing of a face one has met — a recognition of the heart, which is why it names the inward reality the Master hides behind outward practice.", "ولاية (guardianship): from the root و-ل-ي, meaning nearness and to stand close beside. From it come the near protector and the friend who takes charge out of love. ولاية is therefore not distant rule but intimate guardianship — the Imam standing near to guide and shelter."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('the-master-and-the-disciple', 'the boy at the door — limits and conditions', 'student:63473eac132beee0', 2, 'turn toward the one whom you need, but who has no need of you', 'turn toward the one whom you need, but who has no need of you', '<p>The figure the boy must turn toward is not the Master as a private person, but the <strong>rightful owner of the truth</strong> the Master serves — the Imam of the age, the one who holds what God entrusted to His chosen. The Master is a summoner (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">داعي</span>), a deputy who carries the inheritance and opens the door to it. He is not its source, only the hand that guides us to the source.</p><p>The boy already senses the distinction. He offers either to give the people of truth what is theirs directly, or to turn to a representative (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حجّة</span>) lawfully authorized to receive it on their behalf. The Master does not deny either path — he only presses the harder point: the boy still needs to <em>know</em> who possesses the truth, or who stands for him, just as the common people first needed proof of the truth itself. Willingness sets him apart; <strong>not-yet-knowing still binds him to the crowd.</strong></p><p>So how is such a one recognised, when everyone claims the truth and denies it to others? Not by the loudness of a claim, but by three signs the passage lays bare:</p><ul><li><p>The direction of need — the possessor of the truth has no need of the seeker, while the seeker cannot do without him.</p></li><li><p>The preserved inheritance — he holds intact what others received and squandered, like the prosperous brother the impoverished ones must finally come to serve.</p></li><li><p>Proof that stands when tested, accepted through evidence rather than imitation, so the first doubt cannot tear it away.</p></li></ul><p>The final seal of recognition is the covenant (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عهد</span>) — God''s rope stretched across the earth, His secure pledge among His servants. The Master recites it slowly and receives the boy''s commitment, and by accepting it the boy knows with certainty that he has entered the <strong>Party of God</strong>, the company of His devoted friends. Turning toward that figure is submission (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تسليم</span>): we do not manufacture a right that belongs to another, we humble ourselves before the one we need, and we reach him through sincere effort, so that what we receive is earned and firmly held rather than begged as a passing favour.</p>', '["داعي (summoner): from the root د-ع-و, to call or invite. The one who calls others to God''s religion — active, reaching outward. The same root gives دعوة, the summons itself, so the caller and his calling share one breath.", "حجّة (proof / authoritative representative): from the root ح-ج-ج, to argue toward a settled conclusion, the same root behind the pilgrimage حجّ as a directed journey. A حجّة is both a binding argument and the living person who stands as that argument for the Imam.", "عهد (covenant): from the root ع-ه-د, to pledge and to keep in mind. The root joins promising with remembering, for a covenant is a bond you must never let slip from memory — which is why its neglect is treated as betrayal.", "تسليم (submission): from the root س-ل-م, to be whole, safe, at peace. The same root gives سلام and إسلام. Submission is not defeat but entering wholeness — handing oneself over so completely that nothing broken remains."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('the-master-and-the-disciple', 'the boy at the door — limits and conditions', 'student:6f3d6ff2ba609cfc', 3, 'It is the covenant of God.', 'It is the covenant of God.', '<p>The chapter keeps the words of the covenant hidden on purpose, and that silence is itself the first lesson. Something called, in the passage, <strong>God''s rope stretched across the earth</strong> — <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حبل الله</span> — a lifeline lowered to whoever will take hold of it, is not printed for idle eyes; it is spoken, mouth to ear, only to one who has already bound himself to its conditions.</p><p>Still, the boy is not committing to a blank page. Everything the Master said before naming the covenant tells us its substance. To accept it is to pledge these things:</p><ul><li><p>to affirm Allah''s rights over us and gather up the duties we owe Him,</p></li><li><p>to give allegiance (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بيعة</span>) to the rightful possessor of the truth, the one who has no need of us,</p></li><li><p>to guard the secret, holding fast where others let their inheritance slip.</p></li></ul><p>Think of it like the deed to a house handed down from a father. The words on the page matter, but what the deed really transfers is a <em>relationship</em> — a claim, a trust, and a duty to keep. The boy is not memorizing a formula; he is entering a bond with the living guide who carries the truth, and through that guide, with Allah Himself.</p><p>This is why the Master calls it the thing honored on the day the religion was brought to completion:</p><p><span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْيَوْمَ أَكْمَلْتُ لَكُمْ دِينَكُمْ</span> &quot;Today I have perfected for you your religion.&quot; Al-Ma''idah 5:3</p><p>The covenant is our share in that completed gift — outwardly a set of practices and symbols, inwardly the knowledge that gives them life. When the boy weeps as it is recited, he is not reacting to new information. He has felt himself lifted into <strong>the Party of God</strong>, the company of His devoted friends, and a lifeline, once grasped, changes everything about where we now stand.</p>', '["عهد (covenant): from the root ع-ه-د, meaning to keep, tend, and charge something so it is never neglected; the same root gives تعهّد, to take responsibility for and look after. A covenant is thus a trust one keeps returning to and maintaining, not a promise made once and left behind.", "ميثاق (solemn pledge): from the root و-ث-ق, to be firm, secure, and trustworthy; the same root gives وثاق, a binding cord, and وثيقة, a written document. A ميثاق is a pledge tied so tightly it holds fast — a bond you can lean your whole weight upon.", "بيعة (allegiance): from the root ب-ي-ع, to sell and transact, the very root of buying and selling. To give بيعة is to enter a binding exchange, clasping hands as buyer and seller do, offering one''s loyalty and receiving the trust in return.", "حبل (rope): from the root ح-ب-ل, meaning a cord or rope, and by extension a connection between two things. Calling the covenant حبل الله pictures a lifeline let down from above, whole and unbroken, for anyone below to take hold of."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('the-master-and-the-disciple', 'how the world was made', 'student:e81fdd1e315130de', 1, 'whose witnesses are seven kingdoms', 'whose witnesses are seven kingdoms', '<p>The passage counts letters and finds worlds in them. &quot;A will, a command, by a saying&quot; (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إرادة أمر بقول</span>) is twelve letters, and those twelve become twelve signs above and twelve below — the twelve constellations (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">البروج</span>) in the sky and the twelve islands (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الجزائر</span>) on the earth. <strong>The signs match because they spring from the same twelve letters</strong>, one creative word written once in heaven and once in the ground beneath us.</p><p>But the witnesses do not match, and that is the real question. The heaven''s twelve are attested by seven kingdoms — the seven climes (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الأقاليم</span>), the great regions into which the old world was mapped — while the earth''s twelve are attested by seventeen seas. Above, the witnesses are few and gathered; below, many and scattered.</p><p>Picture a single lamp and its reflections. Held up in a still, high room, one flame throws a handful of clean reflections; carried down into a hall of broken mirrors, the same flame splinters into far more. <em>The light has not changed; only the surfaces have.</em> The heaven is the world nearer the inward, where meaning is one and its signs stay gathered; the earth is the outward world, where that same meaning shatters into a crowd of witnesses.</p><p>This is the rule the Master gives as the chapter closes: <strong>the inward is one, and its witnesses in the outward are many</strong>, &quot;because of the abundance of the likenesses and the breadth of the sacred laws.&quot; Seven belongs to the sky because seven is the number of the higher order that governs from above — the seven speakers, the seven heavens. Seventeen belongs to the sea because the outward multiplies; water spreads and divides where light stays single.</p><p>So seven above and seventeen below over the same twelve is no mismatch. We are watching one truth descend — kept whole and few in the heaven of meaning, broken into many bright witnesses on the earth of form.</p>', '["برج (constellation): from the root ب-ر-ج, to rise high and stand out — which also gives the word for a tower. A constellation is a tower of stars, a conspicuous house in the sky that the sun is seen to enter and leave.", "جزيرة (island): from the root ج-ز-ر, to cut off or sever; the same root gives جَزْر, the ebbing of the tide. An island is land the water has cut away and set apart, encircled and defined by the sea around it.", "شاهد (witness): from the root ش-ه-د, to be present and to testify to what one has seen. A witness is not hearsay but something present that vouches for another — the seas and kingdoms stand present, attesting to the signs.", "إقليم (clime, kingdom): a region of the earth, an early borrowing into Arabic from the Greek word for a zone or slope of the world, kept as one of the seven great bands into which the inhabited earth was divided."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('the-master-and-the-disciple', 'how the world was made', 'student:97c51d6b84da1b71', 2, 'That comes to twelve letters', 'That comes to twelve letters', '<p>The puzzle dissolves once we notice that the two counts are counting two different things, not the same phrase twice. Both point back to the very same act of creation; they simply give it two different faces.</p><p>The first count listens to the divine utterance itself. When Allah wills a thing, the word He speaks is <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كُن فَيَكُون</span> — Be, and it is. Spell that spoken word out and it carries seven letters, and so <strong>seven foundational things branch out of it</strong>: the air, then water, darkness and light, then smoke, the condensing of heat, and fire. The seven letters are the seven bones of creation.</p><p>The second count spells out instead the names of the three inner movements behind the utterance — <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِرَادَة</span> (a will), then <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَمْر</span> (a command), then <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">بِقَوْل</span> (by a saying). Written out, those three names carry twelve letters, and so <strong>twelve signs are drawn across the sky</strong>: the twelve constellations, each a marker of one of those twelve letters.</p><p>Think of a single order a captain gives. We can count the letters of the command he shouts, or the letters in the words that name his intention, his instruction, and his speech. It is one event, tallied two ways.</p><p>So there is no contradiction, only one creative act read at two depths: heard as an utterance it yields seven, unfolded into its inner names it yields twelve — seven principles below and twelve signs above, both flowing from the one will that says <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كُن</span>.</p>', '["كُن (Be): from the root ك-و-ن, to be or to come into existence, the command form of كَانَ. The word for the whole cosmos, كَوْن, grows from this same root, so the word that summons existence and the word for existence itself are one family.", "حَرْف (letter): from the root ح-ر-ف, an edge, brink, or margin. A حَرْف is the edge of a sound, its smallest cut, which is why counting حُرُوف is really counting the edges from which larger words are assembled.", "بُرْج (constellation): from the root ب-ر-ج, to be high, prominent, to tower up. A بُرْج is a tower or fortress, so a constellation is read as a tower of light standing tall in the sky — a raised, unmistakable sign."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('the-master-and-the-disciple', 'the world, the hereafter, and the speech of parables', 'student:a4f0501fdff9a354', 1, 'Monday is for the doublings, in the great meaning', 'Monday is for the doublings, in the great meaning', '<p>There is a hidden order in this list, and once we catch it the whole week opens like a lock. Each day is tied to its meaning by the <strong>number folded inside its own Arabic name</strong>, and the count climbs steadily from rest toward gathering.</p><p>Saturday (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">السبت</span>) is named from ceasing and stillness, so it is given to repose. Sunday (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الأحد</span>) carries the word for one, so it holds unity and the single turning of the year. From there the numbers take over:</p><ol><li><p>Monday (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الاثنين</span>) is two, the day of the doublings.</p></li><li><p>Tuesday (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الثلاثاء</span>) is three, given three houses.</p></li><li><p>Wednesday (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الأربعاء</span>) is four, given the four sacred things.</p></li><li><p>Thursday (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الخميس</span>) is five, given the fifth of the causes of Allah.</p></li></ol><p>Friday (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الجمعة</span>) is named from gathering, and so it is the greatest law — the day that draws all the others into one.</p><p>Now the terms the chapter leaves unspoken. The doublings (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">التثنية</span>) are the great <strong>Pair</strong> — not two separate things, but one origin folded into two, the way every rank in creation descends from a first couple. Tuesday''s three houses are the three tiers this chapter later unveils: the outward (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الظاهر</span>), the inward (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الباطن</span>), and the inmost (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">باطن الباطن</span>) — the shell, the white, and the yolk of the guarded egg. The four sacred things are the four sacred months (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الأشهر الحرم</span>) that Allah set apart from the twelve months of the year, withdrawn from ordinary time.</p><p>Al-Tawbah 9:36</p><p>And the fifth of the causes of Allah is the fifth rank among the causes — those luminous intermediaries the chapter names as standing between Allah and His Speakers, counted here to five as a day of five would ask. So nothing in the list is idle: each day''s name announces its number, and the number summons its own sacred set.</p>', '["السبت (the Sabbath): from the root س-ب-ت, to cut off and come to rest. The week''s labour is severed and stillness is what remains — which is why the first day answers to repose rather than to any number in its name.", "الاثنين (Monday): from the root ث-ن-ي, to fold one thing back upon another. The same root yields the doublings (التثنية) — not two separate things but one turned into a pair, so the day of two becomes the day of the great Pair.", "الحرم (the sacred): from the root ح-ر-م, to forbid and set apart. What is sacred is precisely what is fenced off from common use, so the four sacred things (الأشهر الحرم) are the four spans of time withdrawn from the ordinary year.", "الجمعة (Friday): from the root ج-م-ع, to gather and bring together. The day of congregation pulls the scattered worshippers into a single body — which is why it stands as the greatest law, the day that gathers all the rest into itself."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('the-master-and-the-disciple', 'the world, the hereafter, and the speech of parables', 'student:7b9a86e55b29fbed', 2, 'These are the seven and the two orbits', 'These are the seven and the two orbits', '<h3>The seven and the two, and why they sit highest</h3><p>When the boy asks about the seven kingdoms of the heavens and their orbits, the Master answers with a count that seems not to add up: <strong>&quot;the seven and the two orbits.&quot;</strong> He is naming the whole visible order of the sky — the seven turning spheres we track by the wandering stars <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الكواكب السيّارة</span>, and above them two greater orbits that carry everything else and enclose the seven within them.</p><p>Picture our sky as a set of nested glass globes. The inner seven move in plain view; but they all ride inside two vast outer shells — the sphere that holds the fixed stars <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الكواكب الثابتة</span>, and the outermost orbit that turns the entire heavens once each day. Those two are counted apart because they do not merely move among the rest — <strong>they contain the rest</strong>, and to contain a thing is to stand higher than it.</p><h3>Why these are the greatest causes</h3><p>In this teaching a cause (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سبب</span>) is a rung on the ladder between Allah and His creation, and <strong>rank on that ladder is everything</strong>. The seven-and-two belong to the Speaking Imam <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">النّاطق</span>, who stands at the summit of the earthly order. Their height in the sky is a likeness of his height among people: as the two great orbits gather all the lower spheres inside them, the Speaker gathers all the rulings and all the ranks beneath him.</p><h3>How the seven seas differ</h3><p>The seven seas are also called causes, and the boy is right to hear the echo — but the Master sets them &quot;below those first causes.&quot; The heavens are above; the seas rest upon the earth. So the heavens are the causes tying Allah to the Speaker, while <strong>the seas are the causes tying Allah to the imams who come after him</strong>. The difference is not the number seven; it is the level. Height in the world of nature mirrors nearness in the world of rank, and the imam stands one degree below the Speaker exactly as the sea lies one world below the sky.</p>', '["سبب (cause): from the root س-ب-ب, whose first sense is a rope or cord — the one let down to reach water in a well, or gripped to climb. A سبب is therefore a means or link by which a far thing is reached, here a rung connecting the creation upward toward Allah.", "النّاطق (the Speaker): from the root ن-ط-ق, to utter articulate speech. The نَاطِق is the one given speech — the Prophet who voices the revealed law — set against the silent one who keeps and interprets its inner meaning.", "فلك (orbit): from the root ف-ل-ك, tied to roundness and turning; the same root names the whirl of a spindle. A فَلَك is a revolving celestial sphere, named for the circling motion that never rests.", "إمام (imam): from the root أ-م-م, to intend or head toward — the same root that gives أَمَام, \"in front.\" An إِمَام is the one at the front, whom others face and follow, going ahead of them on the way."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('the-master-and-the-disciple', 'the world, the hereafter, and the speech of parables', 'student:86ba748f5b14feb0', 3, 'It is the time of the Will', 'It is the time of the Will', '<p>The Will (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْإِرَادَة</span>) is not Allah weighing a choice the way we weigh ours, turning a matter over until we decide. It is <strong>the single, effortless turning by which He wills a thing into being</strong> — no hesitation, no before-and-after in Him. When the Master sets &quot;time&quot; between the air and Allah and calls it the time of the Will, he points to the sheer moment of that turning, the instant of the command <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كن</span>, &quot;Be.&quot;</p><p>The trap is to hear &quot;time&quot; and picture a clock. <strong>Ordinary time is a measure of change</strong> — the sun moving, a shadow lengthening, a heartbeat. It needs created, moving things to measure, so it cannot run before creation; it is born with the world, not ahead of it.</p><p>The time of the Will only shares the name. It measures nothing created, because nothing created is yet there. It is not the ticking of the spheres but the &quot;when&quot; of an act — every act has its moment, and the Will''s moment is the Word <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كن</span>.</p><p>Consider striking a match in the dark. There is the instant of the strike, and then the flame that burns and can be timed. We cannot measure the strike by the flame''s clock, for before the strike there is no flame to tick. <strong>The strike is what starts the flame''s time; it does not belong to that time.</strong></p><p>So the time of the Will &quot;precedes&quot; the air not by sitting earlier on a line — there is no line yet — but by standing nearer to Allah, above even the greatest created likeness. And it is the time <em>of</em> creation because it is the moment the Command is uttered, where willing and being are one breath:</p><blockquote><p><span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إِنَّمَا أَمْرُهُ إِذَا أَرَادَ شَيْئًا أَنْ يَقُولَ لَهُ كُنْ فَيَكُونُ</span></p><p>His command, when He wills a thing, is only to say to it &quot;Be,&quot; and it is.</p><p>Ya-Sin 36:82</p></blockquote><p>Here the willing (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَرَادَ</span>) and the &quot;Be&quot; (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كُنْ</span>) are not two steps with an interval between them. The Will and its command are one act, and its &quot;time&quot; is that act''s own instant — the threshold at which the hidden passes into the made.</p>', '["إِرَادَة (will): from the root ر-و-د, whose sense is to go to and fro seeking — a رَائِد is the scout sent ahead to find pasture and water. Will, then, is the soul''s seeking-out and settling upon a thing; Allah''s Will is that turning without any lack, His pure inclining toward what shall be.", "مَشِيئَة (will): from the root ش-ي-أ, the very root of شَيْء, \"a thing.\" To will (شَاءَ) is, in its own letters, to make a thing be a thing — so the Will is the granting of reality to what was nothing, the point where a bare possibility is given its thing-hood.", "كُنْ (be): the command form of كَانَ, \"to be,\" from the root ك-و-ن — the same root as كَوْن, \"existence,\" and الْكَوْن, \"the cosmos.\" The single word كُنْ therefore already carries the whole created order within its letters: to say \"Be\" is to open being itself.", "وَقْت (time): from the root و-ق-ت, a bounded and appointed measure — from it comes مِيقَات, the fixed time set for a rite. A وَقْت is never bare duration but a limit assigned to something, which fits a \"time\" that is nothing but the moment of the Command."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('the-master-and-the-disciple', 'three layers of knowledge', 'student:9dc564b3090817da', 1, 'they are the seven appointed speakers', 'they are the seven appointed speakers', '<p>The king''s dream is really a diagram of two ladders — one hung in heaven, one planted on earth — and the sleep-vision can only show them the way the outward always shows things: in shapes that must be read inward.</p><p>The seven lean cows are the seven <strong>speaking prophets</strong> (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">النُّطَقَاء</span>), each one sent with a new law for humankind:</p><ol><li><p>Adam</p></li><li><p>Noah</p></li><li><p>Abraham</p></li><li><p>Moses</p></li><li><p>Jesus</p></li><li><p>Muhammad</p></li><li><p>the awaited one (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">القَائِم</span>) who completes the cycle</p></li></ol><p>They are called lean because they are driven by need — each hungers upward for what descends from Allah through the ranks above him.</p><p>The seven green ears carry a second pairing. Beside each speaker stands a <strong>guardian</strong> (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلِيّ</span>), the foundation (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أَسَاس</span>) who keeps and unfolds what the speaker uttered. In the line we know best, the guardian set beside Muhammad is Maulana Ali, the most trustworthy handhold named earlier in this very conversation.</p><p>So how does a cause (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">سبب</span>) differ from a speaker, when the passage says both stand between Allah and His creation? A cause is the higher, celestial rank — the fat cow that never thins — the unbroken channel of light pouring down from the divine side. The speaker is its earthly mouth: he receives that light and <strong>turns it into human speech and law</strong>, exactly as <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْحَوْل</span>, the Imam, turns the Creator''s speech into the words of the children of Adam. The cause gives; the speaker voices.</p><p>That is why the lean cows eat the fat and the dry ears feed on the green — the earthly rank lives only by drawing on the heavenly one set above it, grace falling ladder-rung by ladder-rung and never once running dry from the hand that sends it.</p>', '["سبب (cause): from the root س-ب-ب, whose oldest sense is the rope let down into a well to draw up water. A سبب is thus a link or means that ties a lower thing to a higher source — so a celestial cause is literally the cord by which grace is drawn down to those below.", "نَاطِق (speaker): from the root ن-ط-ق, to speak or give utterance. A نَاطِق is the one who voices — the prophet who takes the silent inner light and gives it audible body as speech and law, so what was hidden becomes something people can hear and follow.", "وَلِيّ (guardian): from the root و-ل-ي, to be near, to stand next to, to take charge of. A وَلِيّ is one who stands closest and holds guardianship — hence the friend of Allah set nearest the speaker, keeping and interpreting what was given.", "أَسَاس (foundation): from the root أ-س-س, to lay a base or footing. An أَسَاس is the groundwork a building rests on — so the guardian is called this because the whole structure of a prophet''s law stands firm only upon the one who carries and secures its inner meaning."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('the-master-and-the-disciple', 'three layers of knowledge', 'student:874abc577ac52018', 2, 'That is the end of your guidance and the utmost of the worlds', 'That is the end of your guidance and the utmost of the worlds', '<p>The Master answers the boy''s question by refusing to answer it flatly, and that refusal is itself the teaching. He has given the boy a ladder, not a single object, and a ladder is climbed rung by rung, not described from the ground. What the boy can hold in his hand today is the one who stands before him — his guide, the door to the Imam, whom the Master calls his rope and his most trustworthy handhold (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الْعُرْوَة الْوُثْقَى</span>). Above that guide is the Imam, and the Imam is not the rope itself but its <strong>outward sign</strong>, the visible knot of a cord whose far end is anchored out of sight.</p><p>The whole image is drawn from a single command: <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَاعْتَصِمُوا بِحَبْلِ اللَّهِ جَمِيعًا</span> Hold fast, all together, to the rope of Allah. Al Imran 3:103</p><p>Read the Master''s own words closely — he says the seeker is guided <em>by a rope from Allah</em> (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَبْل مِنَ اللَّهِ</span>) <em>to the rope of Allah</em> (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حَبْل اللَّهِ</span>). Those are not two cords but the two ends of one, and the difference between them is direction:</p><ul><li><p>The rope that comes from Allah is guidance let <em>down</em> — the living chain of the Imam, his proof, and his callers, lowered toward us so that a person on the ground has something real to grasp.</p></li><li><p>The rope of Allah is that same cord seen at its <em>upper</em> anchor, tied fast in the divine world where it begins, which is why the Master calls it the end of the boy''s guidance and the utmost of the worlds.</p></li></ul><p>So the seeker climbs from the rope that reaches him to the rope where it is fastened. The Master will not name the far end plainly because <strong>it is a station arrived at, never merely heard</strong>. To describe it would be to hand the boy an outward word for an inward place; the word would be true and still leave him standing exactly where he was. The only way to know what the cord is tied to is to climb it.</p>', '["حَبْل (rope, cord): from the root ح-ب-ل, the sense of binding and tying fast. The same root gives the cord one draws water with and the bond of a covenant, so a rope in this language is never dead fibre but a live tie between two parties — which is why guidance itself can be called a rope: it binds the one below to the one above.", "الْعُرْوَة الْوُثْقَى (the most trustworthy handhold): عُرْوَة, from ع-ر-و, is the loop or handle of a vessel, the part made precisely to be gripped; وُثْقَى, from و-ث-ق, means firmly bound and reliable. Together, the one handhold that will not tear away when the whole weight of a person hangs from it — Al-Baqarah 2:256.", "الْحُجَّة (proof, the one who establishes the argument): from ح-ج-ج, to make for a goal and to argue with a decisive case. The root also gives the pilgrimage (حَجّ), a journey toward a fixed destination, so the حُجَّة is at once the standing evidence of the Imam and the appointed way one travels toward him."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('the-master-and-the-disciple', 'three layers of knowledge', 'student:f925762714ecc642', 3, 'the lord of the twelve captains', 'the lord of the twelve captains', '<p>Think of a single year and how it turns. It is one whole thing, yet completed only across twelve months, and no month stands alone — each carries the year forward until it comes full circle. The speaking Imam is called the <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اَلْحَوْل</span>, the year, for the same reason: he is one, yet <strong>his light reaches creation only through twelve who complete him</strong>.</p><p>Those twelve are the captains, the <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نُقَبَاء</span> — also named the twelve <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُجَج</span> and, together, the <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عُصْبَة</span>, the band of men &quot;possessed of power.&quot; They are not rivals to the Imam and not copies of him. They are the vessels through which his single voice is carried outward, the way twelve months carry the one year.</p><p>Beside the Imam stands one who is foremost among these twelve, and he is the <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُوَّة</span>, the strength. He is the Imam''s own <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُجَّة</span> and his door: <strong>he receives the Creator''s speech from the Imam in summary and hands it down, in detail, to the callers</strong> (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الدُّعَاة</span>), each taking his share according to his place. So the burden of utterance is borne in ranks — from the Imam, to his door, to the twelve, to the callers, and then to the people.</p><p>The Master reads all of this into Joseph''s dream, where Joseph sees the sun, the moon, and eleven planets:</p><p><span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">يَا أَبَتِ إني رأيْتُ أَحَدَ عَشَرَ كَوْكَباً وَالشَّمْسَ وَالقَمَرَ رَأَيْتُهُمْ لِي سَاجِدِيْنَ</span> O my father, I saw eleven planets, and the sun and the moon; I saw them prostrating to me. Yusuf 12:4</p><p>The sun is the Imam; the moon is his <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حُجَّة</span> and his door, the <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">قُوَّة</span>; and the eleven planets, joined to the moon, complete the twelve captains. So the one strengthened at the Imam''s side is himself the first of the twelve — nearest and brightest, as the moon is nearest among the lights of the night.</p>', '["حَوْل (year): from the root ح-و-ل, to turn, shift, or come full circle. A year is a حَوْل because time turns and returns to where it began; the Imam bears the same name because he turns (يحوّل) the hidden speech of the Creator into the plain speech of people — the very same turning.", "قُوَّة (power): from the root ق-و-ي, to be strong. It names the حُجَّة who stands beside the Imam because Allah has strengthened him alone to carry the heavy weight of utterance and pass it on — strength given, not owned.", "نَقِيب (captain): from the root ن-ق-ب, to bore through or search out. A نَقِيب is a chief who sees into the affairs and the inner state of those under him and vouches for them — a leader knit to his people, not set above them.", "حُجَّة (proof): from the root ح-ج-ج, to argue decisively and to aim toward a goal. A حُجَّة is the clinching proof; here it is the man through whom Allah establishes His own argument (حُجَّة) upon creation.", "عُصْبَة (band): from the root ع-ص-ب, to bind tightly, as a sinew or nerve (عَصَب) binds the body. A عُصْبَة is a group knit close together by kinship — here the twelve حُجَج bound around the one Imam.", "دُعَاة (callers): from the root د-ع-و, to call or summon. A داعي (plural دُعَاة) is one who calls people onward — the caller who summons to the Imam and hands down, in detail, the teaching he receives."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('the-master-and-the-disciple', 'the five shares and the long road to the shaykh', 'student:cde6aa2afb6d7d07', 1, 'bring out a fifth of the best of it', 'bring out a fifth of the best of it', '<p>The customary alms most people picture takes a small slice — a fortieth — from wealth that has sat and grown across a whole year. What the Master asks for here is different in size and in spirit: a fifth (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">خمس</span>), and not just any fifth but the dearest portion, the part we would least like to let go. <strong>The measure is meant to be a stretch</strong>, because a gift only purifies the giver when it costs him something he loves.</p><p>This fifth carries a name and a history of its own. In the Quran a fifth of what comes into a believer''s hands is set apart for Allah, for His Messenger, and for the near of kin:</p><p><span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَاعْلَمُوا أَنَّمَا غَنِمْتُم مِّن شَيْءٍ فَأَنَّ لِلَّهِ خُمُسَهُ وَلِلرَّسُولِ وَلِذِي الْقُرْبَىٰ</span> And know that whatever you gain, a fifth of it belongs to Allah, and to the Messenger, and to the near of kin. Al-Anfal 8:41</p><p>The near of kin (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ذوي القربى</span>) are the household of the Prophet, and for us the living hand that receives on their behalf is the Imam of the age — &quot;the one whose claim upon you is binding.&quot; Notice that the Master refuses the share for himself; he calls it a trust the boy must keep <em>until you meet him, for he is the one who holds authority over you and on your behalf.</em></p><p>So when the boy divides everything he owns into five parts, the passage is acting out its own lesson. One part is this purifying fifth; the rest he sorts into thanksgiving, expiation for what he squandered, care for the poor among his brothers, and a gift to his teacher. <strong>Purification is not a fee we pay to be left alone</strong> — it is the moment wealth stops being ours and begins pointing us toward the One, and the one, it truly belongs to.</p><p>Two ideas live inside the word <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">زكاة</span>, and they explain why the fifth is framed as a cleansing rather than a loss: it means both to grow and to be pure. What we release is what makes everything left behind clean, the way cutting back the finest branch is what lets the whole tree bear.</p>', '["خمس (a fifth): from the root خ-م-س, the number five. The word names one part out of five set aside as a due — so \"a fifth\" is not a metaphor here but the plain arithmetic of the obligation, the giver keeping four parts and surrendering the fifth.", "زكاة (purifying alms): from the root ز-ك-و, which joins two ideas the Arabic holds as one — to grow and to be pure. The alms is called this because parting with a portion is what makes the rest wholesome, and what is given cleanly is also what increases; from the same root come تزكية, the purifying of the soul, and زكي, one who is pure.", "قربى (near kinship): from the root ق-ر-ب, to draw near or be close. From it come قُرب, nearness, and قريب, a close relative. ذوي القربى are therefore \"those of nearness\" — the kin nearest the Prophet, whose closeness of blood mirrors a closeness of standing."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('the-master-and-the-disciple', 'the five shares and the long road to the shaykh', 'student:ae8bdd920e1dfb5b', 2, 'he is the one who holds authority over you and on your behalf', 'he is the one who holds authority over you and on your behalf', '<p>The Master never speaks the name, and that silence is itself part of the lesson. The one &quot;who holds authority over you and on your behalf&quot; is <strong>the Imam of the age</strong> (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إمام</span>) — the living guardian whose right Allah has made binding upon every believer. When the Master says to keep the shares as a trust &quot;until you meet him,&quot; he tells the boy that the fifth he set aside was never the Master''s to take; it belongs to the one in whose hand are the keys of the kingdom.</p><p>Notice how carefully the boy''s wealth is sorted. The Master returns his own share, leaves the brothers'' share with the boy, and turns the zakat and the thanksgiving into <strong>a trust</strong> (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أمانة</span>) — carried safely and handed over whole to its true owner, the Imam. This is why the boy cannot simply give the shares away: to deliver them anywhere but into the Imam''s authority would break the trust.</p><p>The journey passes through three figures, and <strong>each stands a rung higher than the last</strong>:</p><ol><li><p>The Master is a servant and a son of the house, the caller who first opens the door.</p></li><li><p>The Shaykh, the greater scholar, completes what the Master began and names the boy anew.</p></li><li><p>The Imam is the source both of them serve — the cause behind the signs, from whom their own light is borrowed.</p></li></ol><p>So the Master and the Shaykh are not rivals to the Imam but his hands. The Master says it plainly: &quot;I am his son and one of his servants.&quot; Even the Shaykh, for all his majesty, sends the boy onward rather than keeping him. <strong>Every guide in the story points beyond himself</strong> to the one who was never named — a lamp is honored not for its own flame but for the light it carries.</p><p>And here is the tenderness the passage wants us to feel: the boy''s ache to be taken &quot;to the door of his chamber&quot; is the soul''s longing for its true guardian (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ولاية</span>), the nearness in which all our striving at last comes to rest.</p>', '["إمام (leader): from the root أ-م-م, to head toward or intend. The same root gives أُمّ (mother) and أمام (in front). An imam is the one at the front whom others face and follow, as worshippers align behind the one who leads the prayer.", "ولاية (guardianship): from the root و-ل-ي, meaning nearness and closeness. From it come وليّ (near protector) and مولى (patron). It is not distant rule but the binding nearness of a guardian who stands closest to us and acts on our behalf.", "أمانة (trust): from the root أ-م-ن, to be safe and secure, the same root as إيمان (faith) and أمين (trustworthy). A thing given in trust is placed where it is safe and returned whole; faith itself is a trust the believer is kept safe in keeping.", "زكاة (alms): from the root ز-ك-و, to grow and to be pure. Giving it both cleanses wealth of its impurity and makes it increase, so that what is set aside for its rightful owner purifies all the rest."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('the-master-and-the-disciple', 'the five shares and the long road to the shaykh', 'student:27374b3c8d356153', 3, 'he is the cause behind the signs', 'he is the cause behind the signs', '<p>To call this figure &quot;the cause behind the signs&quot; is to say that the outward marks of religion — the verses, the miracles, the visible tokens Allah scatters through the world — do not explain themselves. Someone living must stand behind them and give them their meaning. The signs meant here are the <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آيات</span>, the verses and tokens that point beyond their own surface, and the one who stands behind them is the Imam (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إمام</span>). <strong>He is the reason a sign becomes readable at all</strong> — without him it stays a locked door, however beautiful.</p><p>Picture a night sky crowded with stars. Every one is real and shining, yet a scattered field of light tells us nothing until someone traces the constellations and names the road home. The Imam is that tracer of meaning: the <em> <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">آيات</span></em> remain overhead for everyone, but through him they turn into a path we can actually walk.</p><p>The light of Sinai reaches back to Musa. When he came to the sacred valley he did not receive dry words; he saw a <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نور</span>, a light, and from within it Allah spoke to him. At the Mount, Allah showed a power the mountain itself could not bear:</p><p><span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">فَلَمَّا تَجَلَّىٰ رَبُّهُ لِلْجَبَلِ جَعَلَهُ دَكًّا</span> And when his Lord manifested to the mountain, He made it crumble to dust. Al-A''raf 7:143</p><p>So Sinai names the place where divine light once broke into the world through a single chosen man. <strong>To swear &quot;by the light of Sinai&quot; that this figure is the cause behind the signs is to set him exactly where Musa stood</strong> — the point where a seeker meets the light of Allah and is taught by it. The Master is telling the boy, gently, that the one he is being led toward is no scholar among scholars but the living Sinai of his own age: the face turned toward Allah from which our own light is completed, and the hand that unlocks the meaning of every sign.</p>', '["آية (sign, verse): from the root ء-ي-ي, meaning a clear mark or token by which a thing is recognized. A verse of the Quran and a wonder in creation share the one word because each is a signpost — something set up so that we notice it and are led through it to the One who placed it.", "نور (light): from the root ن-و-ر, to shine and give light. The same root carries نار, fire — light that both guides and warms. It names not raw brightness alone but light that shows a path, which is why revelation and guidance are so often spoken of as نور.", "الطور (the Mount): from the root ط-و-ر, carrying the sense of a boundary or a stage passed through — a طور is also a phase in a person''s growth. The mountain of revelation is thus a threshold, the edge where one state of the seeker ends and a higher one begins."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('the-master-and-the-disciple', 'the five shares and the long road to the shaykh', 'student:45138f7dd4e1ee23', 4, 'until he reached his own greater father', 'until he reached his own greater father', '<p>When the Master leaves the boy and travels to his &quot;greater father,&quot; then later brings the boy to a &quot;greater scholar,&quot; and that scholar turns out to be &quot;the Shaykh, the lord of the house,&quot; these are not three strangers passing through the story. They are one elder — the Master''s own teacher and spiritual father. <strong>The single figure wears three names because each catches a different angle on him:</strong> father names the bond, scholar names what he carries, and Shaykh (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">شيخ</span>) names his rank as one ripened enough to guide.</p><p>The account works by a kind of spiritual fathering, where a guide &quot;fathers&quot; the one he brings to the truth. So the Master is a father to the boy, and a son to this greater elder above him. What we watch is a <strong>ladder of teachers</strong>, each standing on the shoulder of the one before, each addressed as a father — the very way our own guide can become our father though another man gave us birth.</p><p>Above every elder on this ladder stands one who is not a rung on it at all — <strong>the one who &quot;holds authority,&quot; the Imam (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">إمام</span>).</strong> He is the person to whom the boy''s zakat is owed and kept in trust, the one in whose hand are &quot;the keys of the gardens.&quot; The Shaykh never claims that place; he only points to it. When the Master calls himself &quot;his son and one of his servants,&quot; the &quot;he&quot; is this Imam, the source the whole chain draws from.</p><p>So the figures line up in an ascending order:</p><ol><li><p>The Master — the boy''s first guide and nearest father.</p></li><li><p>The greater father, the greater scholar, the Shaykh of the house — one elder, the Master''s own teacher and the gate that leads higher.</p></li><li><p>The Imam — who holds authority over all of them, and to whom their service finally returns.</p></li></ol><p>The story keeps the Imam veiled on purpose. <em>We are never handed his name.</em> The boy is led up step by step, and the highest station is spoken of in light and image — Sinai, the keys, the opened breast — rather than pointed at plainly, because <strong>nearness to it is earned by the climb, not by being told.</strong></p>', '["شيخ (elder): from the root ش-ي-خ, carrying the sense of advancing in age and growing venerable. A شيخ is not merely an old man but one ripened by years and knowledge, so the word names a guide whose authority is his very maturity — exactly the elder the boy is at last brought up to meet.", "إمام (leader): from the root أ-م-م, to head toward and to intend. The same root gives أمام, meaning in front. An إمام is the one who stands ahead and whom others face and follow, so authority here is pictured as being at the very head of the line the community walks.", "وليّ (one who holds authority): from the root و-ل-ي, whose core sense is nearness, to be close with nothing standing between. Guardianship and authority grow from that closeness, for the one truly near a matter is the one entitled to manage it — hence the Imam, nearest of all, stands over the boy''s affair on his behalf."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('the-master-and-the-disciple', 'the five shares and the long road to the shaykh', 'student:9cca95b281568d0c', 5, 'The name belongs to you, and you belong to the name', 'The name belongs to you, and you belong to the name', '<p>Notice the trap the Shaykh gently springs. The boy first offers his given name, then declares himself free, as though a name were a thing he could hold up and own. But a name (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">اسم</span>) is not something we grip in our hand; it is <strong>a mark placed upon us by another</strong>, and its whole purpose is to point past itself to the one who conferred it.</p><p>So the Shaykh presses the question home: who freed you? The Master freed you. And if the Master himself is owned and possesses nothing of his own, then his gift of freedom is empty. The boy falls silent, reaching for something no created thing can hold — a name that answers to no one above it.</p><p>Here is the double truth the Shaykh lays bare: <strong>the name belongs to the boy, and the boy belongs to the name</strong> — two bonds, not one.</p><ul><li><p>The name belongs to him because he carries it, is called by it, and is known through it.</p></li><li><p>He belongs to the name because it binds him to duties he did not invent and to a Master he did not appoint.</p></li></ul><p>His own name says it aloud — <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عبد الله</span>, the servant of Allah. To be a servant (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عبد</span>) is already to belong, not to own.</p><p>And now the sharp saying. To claim a name as simply mine, a thing I possess and command with no one above me, is to sit in the seat of the <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">معبود</span>, the worshipped one — for only the One who gives every name owns names absolutely and belongs to no other. A being who was born, who received his name as a gift, cannot own it in that way <strong>without pretending to be a god</strong>. This is why the boy must wait to be named, receiving it as a trust from the one who holds authority over him — the true name of his rebirth, handed to him, never seized.</p>', '["اسم (name): from the root س-م-و, which carries the sense of height and rising, the same root behind سماء, the sky. A name is what is raised up over a thing so it can be called and known — lifted above it like a banner, given from without, not grown from within.", "عبد (servant): from the root ع-ب-د, to serve, to worship, to belong utterly to a master. The same root gives عبادة, worship. To be an عبد is to be owned in devotion — which is why the boy''s name already answers the Shaykh''s question before he speaks.", "معبود (worshipped one): the passive form from that same root ع-ب-د, meaning the one who is served and worshipped. It is the exact mirror of عبد: where the servant belongs, the معبود is belonged to, owning absolutely and owned by none.", "حرّ (free): from the root ح-ر-ر, to be free, and also to be pure or noble. The boy claims to be حرّ, but the root''s dignity is a status conferred by a true owner''s release — never a thing one simply declares of oneself."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('the-master-and-the-disciple', 'the five shares and the long road to the shaykh', 'student:a6ab3b112aba8095', 6, 'circled the ancient House, completed his rites with remembrance', 'circled the ancient House, completed his rites with remembrance', '<p>When the boy releases his pilgrim state, circles the ancient House (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">البيت العتيق</span>), and completes his pilgrimage by the greatest sign, he has not crossed a single desert. He is sitting on the floor in front of the Shaykh, being taught. The pilgrimage described here is <strong>the journey of recognition</strong>, and its whole road is travelled inside him.</p><p>Notice what the passage sets right beside these rites — that he came to know his Lord. The knowing is the arrival. To circle the House is to gather our whole self around the source of divine knowledge, and to take hold of the firm rope is to hold fast to the one who guides us there. The stones of a far city point to something, and here the boy has reached <strong>the very thing they point to</strong>.</p><p>And the Master says so himself, at the chapter''s close, when he hands the boy the rule of everything he has learned: take all these outward forms and turn them into parables (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">أمثال</span>), the praiseworthy ones standing for the truth and its people. So the pilgrimage is not an ornament dropped into the story. It is <strong>the first worked example</strong> of the exact method he is being handed — an outward act carried home to its inner meaning, which is what <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تأويل</span> means.</p><p>Think of it the way we might think of a letter that has finally reached the hand it was written for. The envelope, the stamps, and the long miles are all real, yet not one of them is the message. The rites are the envelope, and the boy has opened it and read what was inside — and once we have read the letter, we do not keep travelling to the post office. This does not erase the outward journey for those who make it; Allah still commands His House be visited:</p><p><span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَلْيَطَّوَّفُوا بِالْبَيْتِ الْعَتِيقِ</span> And let them circle the ancient House. Al-Hajj 22:29</p><p>But for this boy the outward rite has done its work by leading him to its meaning, and that meaning is a living guide, not a place on a map.</p>', '["حج (pilgrimage): from the root ح-ج-ج, to aim deliberately at a destination and set out for it. The same root gives حجة, a proof or argument — so the pilgrim is one who makes purposefully for a goal, and knowledge itself is a kind of aiming at the truth.", "أمثال (parables): the singular مثل is from the root م-ث-ل, to resemble or stand in the place of another. A parable sets one visible thing up so that it stands for something unseen — exactly what the Master asks the boy to do with every outward rite.", "تأويل (esoteric interpretation): from the root أ-و-ل, to return or go back to a beginning. It means carrying an outward form back to its first, inner meaning — the way a branching word is traced home to its root.", "العتيق (ancient): from the root ع-ت-ق, carrying the joined senses of great age, of being freed (as in عتق, the freeing of a servant), and of nobility. The ancient House is at once old, liberated, and honored.", "إحرام (pilgrim state): from the root ح-ر-م, to forbid and to make sacred. To enter it is to cross into a state where ordinary permitted things are set apart as forbidden, so the whole person is consecrated to the goal ahead."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('the-master-and-the-disciple', 'homecoming, the father, and the debate with abu malik', 'student:8c4f4d24a7fa3da0', 1, 'let no eagerness move you to impose تَعْزِير upon them', 'let no eagerness move you to impose تَعْزِير upon them', '<p>When the Master tells Salih to hold back from <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تعزير</span>, he is naming a real tool of the teacher and the judge — a measured correction laid on someone who has slipped, meant to restrain them from doing the same again. It is not a fixed penalty written down for a crime; it is left to the guide''s discretion, shaped to the particular person and moment.</p><p>The startling thing is where the word comes from. Its root reaches in two directions at once — toward holding someone back, and toward lifting someone up. <strong>The very root that means to discipline also means to support and strengthen</strong>, which tells us that the point of correction is never to wound but to prop a person upright when they are leaning toward a fall.</p><p>That is why the Master warns against reaching for it here. Abu Malik and his companions have only just softened; their hearts have turned toward the good after a lifetime of the old creed. A newly turned heart is like a young shoot — press it too hard and it snaps. So the Master says to test them by leaving them to themselves, without harshness, because a seeker who came freely has to be won freely.</p><p>There is a warning about the guide''s own soul folded in as well. Notice the exact words: let no eagerness move you. The danger is not correction itself but the <em>appetite</em> for it — the quiet pleasure of standing over another and setting them straight. When we correct out of eagerness we serve ourselves; when we correct out of mercy we serve the one corrected.</p><p>So the Master hands Salih gentler instruments instead:</p><ul><li><p>Leave them to themselves, so their own sincerity can show.</p></li><li><p>Deal with them kindly, in due season, rather than all at once.</p></li><li><p>Trust that one who inclines toward good is not hidden, and that Allah will make it plain.</p></li></ul><p>The lesson reaches far past this one scene. Whenever we are given a little authority over another''s growth — a child, a student, a friend finding their way — a readiness to punish is usually the surest sign that we are not yet ready to guide.</p>', '["تعزير (discretionary correction): from the root ع-ز-ر, which carries two joined senses at once — to restrain and discipline, and to aid, honor, and strengthen. The same root yields both the corrective chastisement of تعزير and the meaning of backing someone up, so the correction it names is meant to reinforce a person, not merely to punish them."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('the-master-and-the-disciple', 'homecoming, the father, and the debate with abu malik', 'student:2548d6960d69a927', 2, 'whom they had named — for his scrupulousness, the depth of his forbea…', 'whom they had named — for his scrupulousness, the depth of his forbearance', '<p>When a community wants to say that one of its own is the greatest scholar it has, it rarely invents a fresh word for it — it reaches for a name everyone already reveres and lays it over him like a robe. That is exactly what these people do with Abd al-Jabbar Abu Malik. His given name is his own, but the title <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كعب الأحبار</span> is <em>borrowed glory</em> — the name of a scholar so famous for learning that to be called by it is to be called the very summit of the learned.</p><p>The word <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">الأحبار</span> is where the meaning lives. It is the plural of <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حبر</span>, the word for a religious doctor — the rabbi, the master of scripture, the one whose knowledge runs so deep it stains the page like ink. So <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">كعب الأحبار</span> means something closer to the <strong>chief of the scholars</strong>, the reference point against which the others measure themselves. When the passage says they named him this for his scrupulousness, his forbearance, and his defense of his religion, it is naming the three things a people prize in a teacher: that he is careful, that he is patient, and that he will stand guard over what they hold dear.</p><p>Now picture the weight of that title on the day they come to him. They do not walk up to just anyone; they walk up to the man they themselves have crowned as their standard, and they promise in advance that they will not overstep whatever he decides. His word is meant to be <strong>the ceiling of their thought</strong>. And here is the quiet ache of the story: the very title that makes him their safest anchor is what makes his coming change so perilous to them, for the head of the learned is about to sit before a boy and discover that he is only a beginner in the search.</p>', '["الأحبار (the learned, the rabbis): plural of حبر, from the root ح-ب-ر, which carries the ideas of adorning and of ink (حِبْر). A حبر is a scholar whose learning beautifies and whose knowledge marks the page like ink, so the plural names the entire class of religious masters.", "كعب (Kab): from the root ك-ع-ب, meaning to rise up and stand prominent — the same root gives the anklebone that swells above the foot and the raised cube of the كعبة. As a name it signals height and eminence, so كعب الأحبار reads as the eminence of the scholars.", "الورع (scrupulousness): from the root و-ر-ع, meaning to hold back and restrain oneself. The scrupulous person reins in his own appetite and steps away from anything doubtful, so the word names a piety measured by what one refuses rather than by what one takes.", "الحلم (forbearance): from the root ح-ل-م, meaning gentleness, self-possession, and the patience that does not rush to anger. The same root gives the word for a dream (حُلم), as though the forbearing mind stays as calm and unhurried as one who sleeps."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('the-master-and-the-disciple', 'homecoming, the father, and the debate with abu malik', 'student:cb65badff75fd198', 3, 'Salih today is my father, and I am the son of Salih; and Salih is wit…', 'Salih today is my father, and I am the son of Salih; and Salih is with his Lord', '<p>When the father says the son has become his father, he is pointing to a second kind of birth. Our first father gives us a body, and a body is on loan — it grows old and returns to the dust. But the one who wakes a sleeping soul, who leads a heart to the knowledge of Allah, gives a life that does not end. That is the <strong>truer fatherhood</strong>, and by it the boy who guided his own father has become, in the things that last, his father''s father.</p><p>The boy''s name carries the whole meaning. Salih (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">صالح</span>) is simply &quot;the righteous one,&quot; from a root that means to be sound and to set right. So &quot;Salih is my father&quot; reads twice over: the person named Salih, and the Righteous Guide as such — the one who is himself put right and puts others right.</p><p>This is why al-Bakhtari refuses the word death. When Abu Malik later fears the boy has died, the father answers that Salih will endure as long as time itself endures. He speaks now not of a single young man but of what Salih has become — a friend of Allah (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ولي</span>), a living door held open between heaven and earth, the standing proof that <strong>guidance is never withdrawn from the world</strong>.</p><p>Picture a candle lighting another candle. The first flame loses nothing, the light passes on undimmed, and one can go on lighting candles down a long hall without ever spending it. The Righteous One is that light: persons carry it and pass, but <strong>the office itself never goes dark</strong> — the earth, this book teaches, is never left for the blink of an eye without the just one of Allah upon it, whether he stands in the open or is veiled in fear.</p><p>So &quot;Salih is with his Lord&quot; is not a death notice but its opposite. It means the boy has been <strong>joined to the source of that light</strong> — to the Master who first sent the stranger, and beyond him to Allah. Three ordinary things have quietly turned around:</p><ul><li><p>the son has become the father,</p></li><li><p>the created has become the enduring,</p></li><li><p>and a name has become a living presence.</p></li></ul>', '["صالح (the righteous one): from the root ص-ل-ح, to be sound, whole, and in good repair. The same root gives إصلاح, the mending of what is broken. So a صالح is not only one who is himself set right, but one whose work is to set others right — exactly what the boy does for his father.", "ولي (friend, guardian): from the root و-ل-ي, meaning nearness and drawing close; from it also come والٍ, one placed in charge, and مولى, master or ward. The ولي of Allah is the near one, kept close to Him and set as a guardian over others — the living guide who does not die.", "ربّ (Lord): from the root ر-ب-ب, to nurture, to raise, and to have charge of; the same root stands behind تربية, the rearing of a child. To say Salih is with his ربّ echoes the fatherhood at the heart of the passage — he has returned to the One who raises and sustains him."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('the-master-and-the-disciple', 'homecoming, the father, and the debate with abu malik', 'student:e82bcdb28eee5772', 4, 'the Lord is present here upon the earth, and this Lord has a servant…', 'the Lord is present here upon the earth, and this Lord has a servant through whom one is brought to Him', '<p>When al-Bakhtari says his son is &quot;with his Lord,&quot; the word underneath is <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span> — and a <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span> is not only the Creator. In its plainest sense it means the one who raises, nurtures, and brings a thing to its completion, the way a gardener is the <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">رب</span> of his garden. Two very different meanings sit folded inside one small word, and prying them apart is the whole knot Abu Malik is caught in.</p><p>The first meaning is Allah — the God the rest of this chapter labors to describe as <strong>beyond all reach</strong>, higher than any eye, wider than any thought, too great even for attributes. No servant stands between us and Him as His equal, and nothing is like Him. When Salih later insists that Allah cannot be grasped by measurement or by inventing likenesses, this is the God he means.</p><p>The second meaning is the guardian of Allah upon the earth — the Imam, the <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ولي</span>, the one Salih names the just one and the <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">حجّة</span>, the proof. He is a &quot;lord&quot; only in that nurturing sense: <strong>he is the one who carries Allah''s command to us and lifts us toward Him</strong>. He is not a second god. He is himself a servant, chosen and inspired, the face Allah turns toward creation so that the utterly hidden becomes reachable.</p><p>Picture a king so exalted that no one may enter his presence or even look upon him. He appoints a trusted deputy who lives among the people, speaks in his name, and carries their needs back to him. Obeying the deputy is obeying the king; the deputy is never the king, yet he is the only door to him.</p><p>That is why the chapter keeps arguing that the earth is never left empty of such a one:</p><ul><li><p>Allah is too high to be reached directly,</p></li><li><p>so His command must arrive through a chosen just one,</p></li><li><p>and each rank is reached through the rank just below it — a servant through whom one comes to the master, and through the master to Allah.</p></li></ul><p>So Salih being &quot;with his Lord&quot; means he lives in the shelter of that living guide, and through the guide is carried, at last, to the God no sight can hold.</p>', '["رب: from the root ر-ب-ب, to nurture, own, and bring a thing to completion. A رب takes something from lack to fullness, which is why the one word carries both \"master/owner\" and the divine \"Sustainer\" — the sense the story leans on when it calls the guardian a lord.", "ولي: from the root و-ل-ي, meaning nearness and proximity. A ولي is one drawn so close that he takes charge of another''s affairs — hence guardian, friend, and ally at once. The Imam is Allah''s ولي because he stands nearest to His command.", "حجّة: from the root ح-ج-ج, to aim toward a destination and to argue toward a conclusion (the pilgrimage حج shares it). A حجّة is the decisive proof that settles a dispute — here, the just one set between Allah and creation as evidence for them and against them."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('the-master-and-the-disciple', 'homecoming, the father, and the debate with abu malik', 'student:03e42b79f9e5dedb', 5, 'The word one (وَاحِد) is four letters, and we see two of them joined…', 'The word one (وَاحِد) is four letters, and we see two of them joined and two of them standing apart', '<p>Abu Malik has just declared that Allah is one (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَاحِد</span>), saying it as though pronouncing the word were the same as knowing the thing it names. Salih stops him at the word itself. <strong>Look at what you are holding: a written word, four letters, two of them joined and two standing apart.</strong> If the word itself were the oneness, then which letter is the one — the joined pair, the separate pair, or all four together? Once the question is asked, it is plain that what Abu Malik grips is not one at all; it is a little crowd of marks.</p><p>A word is a sign, and a sign is never the thing it points to. Fire is hot, but the word does not burn; bread fills, but the word does not feed. In the same way the word that names oneness is itself made of many, and so <strong>it cannot itself be the oneness</strong> — it can only point toward it. Abu Malik supposed he possessed Allah''s unity because he could say it, when all he possessed was the pointer.</p><p>And a pointer is useless to a man who cannot say where it points. If someone can carry the word yet cannot tell which part of it holds the meaning, then <strong>he has never travelled from the name to the Named.</strong> His oneness was borrowed and recited, imitated from others, never truly known.</p><p>This is why four small letters can topple so large a claim:</p><ul><li><p>the word for one is itself many, so the sign is not the meaning;</p></li><li><p>a sign only points, so it must be read by someone who knows;</p></li><li><p>reading it rightly needs a guide who already knows the Named.</p></li></ul><p>That last step is where Salih is quietly leading. If the bare word cannot carry us across to Allah, then the way to Allah must run through a living guide — <strong>the just one, the friend of Allah, who stands between the name and the Named</strong> and teaches the seeker how to read the sign. Abu Malik''s silence over <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وَاحِد</span> is the door swinging open onto that need.</p>', '["وَاحِد (one): from the root و-ح-د, to be alone, single, with nothing beside it. The وَاحِد is what stands utterly by itself. Salih''s thrust lands precisely here — the written وَاحِد is four letters huddled together, the very opposite of the aloneness the root is meant to name.", "توحيد (declaring oneness): the same root و-ح-د in its intensive form, the act of holding a thing to be single and stripping away every partner and likeness. It is not merely to utter the word one, but to know the One — which naming without knowing never manages to do.", "اسم (name): from the root س-م-و, to rise or be lifted high (whence also مُسمّى, the named). A name is a marker raised up over a thing to point to it, a sign held aloft and never the thing itself — which is exactly why a name can be possessed while the named stays wholly unknown."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('the-master-and-the-disciple', 'homecoming, the father, and the debate with abu malik', 'student:7401adb775b668c5', 6, 'the seven creations of the unseen as the virtuous one himself', 'the seven creations of the unseen as the virtuous one himself', '<p>In this exchange Abu Malik presses on a fine point: the virtuous one, who is the prophet, the <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">نَاطِق</span>, set forth the seven creations of the unseen, and no one else could match him in it. The unseen (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">غَيْب</span>) here means everything the eye cannot reach, the hidden order by which Allah brought the worlds into being before any human witnessed it.</p><p>The seven creations are the <strong>stages of that hidden making</strong>. The spiritual world (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">عَالَم رُوحَانِي</span>) was brought forth from sheer nothingness, not shaped upon any earlier material, and out of it the ranks of creation unfold in their proper order. To name these stages, to say what was made first and what came after, is not a thing anyone can reason out, because no one stood watching when Allah created — a point Salih makes plainly earlier in this chapter.</p><p>That is why <strong>only the prophet may set them forth</strong>. His right to speak of the unseen comes from revelation (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَنْزِيل</span>), the Book sent down to him, and that revelation is the very proof of his truthfulness. In the logic of the passage:</p><ul><li><p>The virtuous one unveils the seven creations by revelation, and no one can bring the like of it except from Allah.</p></li><li><p>The lesser one, his interpreter, sends down no Book of his own, yet he too holds the unseen, by the interpretation (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تَأْوِيل</span>) of what was revealed.</p></li><li><p>Both the sending down and its interpretation come from Allah alone, and are reached only through inspiration.</p></li></ul><p>So the seven creations of the unseen are not a riddle to be cracked by cleverness. They are a <strong>trust</strong>, opened first by the prophet who receives them and then unfolded by his interpreter who draws out their inner meaning. This is why the passage treats knowing them as the mark of one truly sent by Allah, never the mark of one who is only imitating.</p>', '["غَيْب (the unseen): from the root غ-ي-ب, to be hidden or absent from sight. What is غَيْب is not unreal, only out of reach of the eye; so the word names the whole hidden order of creation that lies beyond what our senses can witness.", "نَاطِق (the speaker): from the root ن-ط-ق, to speak or articulate clearly. The prophet is called نَاطِق because he is the one given voice to utter the revelation aloud, turning the silent, hidden order into words a community can receive.", "تَنْزِيل (sending down): from the root ن-ز-ل, to descend or come down. The Book is a تَنْزِيل because it is brought down from Allah to the prophet; the root pictures the higher knowledge descending to the one appointed to receive it.", "تَأْوِيل (interpretation): from the root أ-و-ل, to return a thing to its origin. تَأْوِيل is not free guessing but leading an outward word back to the first meaning it came from, which is the work of the interpreter who unfolds what the revelation holds within."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('the-master-and-the-disciple', 'homecoming, the father, and the debate with abu malik', 'student:0bb82a3342293a5d', 7, 'the lesser one likewise has set himself apart from others by the know…', 'the lesser one likewise has set himself apart from others by the knowledge of the unseen in the interpretation', '<p>When Salih speaks of the virtuous one and the lesser one, he is naming two ranks that Allah appoints to carry His guidance, not two teachers who happened to learn different things. The virtuous one is the <strong>prophet</strong>, the Speaker (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">ناطق</span>) who receives the revelation as it is sent down (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تنزيل</span>) and delivers the outward law to the people.</p><p>The lesser one is the <strong>Imam</strong>, the trustee (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وصي</span>) who receives the inner meaning of that same revelation, its interpretation (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تأويل</span>), and unlocks what the prophet brought. Note the passage''s own care: both the sending down and its interpretation come from Allah, and each reaches its bearer only through inspiration (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">وحي</span>). Neither man invents his knowledge; both draw it from the same unseen well.</p><p>Why call the second one lesser at all? Not because his inspiration is weaker or his reward smaller — the obedient one and the virtuous one share a single reward. He is lesser only in this: he obeys the virtuous one and <strong>stands in need of him</strong>, the way a lamp needs the flame that was lit before it. Picture a king who sends a sealed letter and, with it, the one servant who alone can break the seal — the letter is honored, but it stays shut until the reader speaks.</p><p>So we recognize each not by his claim but by the light he carries:</p><ul><li><p>The prophet is known by the knowledge of the unseen in the Book he brings.</p></li><li><p>The Imam is known by the knowledge of the unseen in the interpretation he gives.</p></li><li><p>The Book itself bears witness to that interpretation, so the two can never contradict.</p></li></ul><p>This is why <strong>no one can counterfeit either rank</strong>. A person cannot forge the revelation, for no one brings its like except from Allah; nor can he forge the interpretation, for it too descends by inspiration and the revealed Book confirms it. The one opens the outer, the other the inner, and they are joined like a word and the meaning hidden inside it.</p>', '["فاضل (the virtuous one): from the root ف-ض-ل, meaning excess, surplus, and superiority. The فاضل is the one who carries the surplus of merit, so the word itself names his precedence in rank over the one who follows him.", "تنزيل (revelation): from the root ن-ز-ل, to descend or come down. تنزيل is the causing-to-descend of the Book from above onto the prophet — the outward wording sent down for all people to receive.", "تأويل (interpretation): from the root أ-و-ل, to return a thing to its origin. تأويل leads the outward word back to the first meaning it came from, which is why the Imam''s task is to return the revelation to its inner sense.", "وحي (inspiration): from the root و-ح-ي, to signal swiftly and cast a meaning in secret. وحي is knowledge thrown straight into the heart without the slow labor of ordinary learning, and both ranks receive their light this way."]');
INSERT INTO companion_note (slug, anchor_key, note_id, idx, title, quote, body_html, etymology) VALUES ('the-master-and-the-disciple', 'homecoming, the father, and the debate with abu malik', 'student:d20857281cf37910', 8, 'the guardians of Allah have kept themselves hidden from you, by تقيّة', 'the guardians of Allah have kept themselves hidden from you, by تقيّة', '<p>Taqiyya (<span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تقيّة</span>) is a <strong>protective concealment</strong> — a guarding of the truth and of those who carry it, so that something precious is not thrown away on people not yet ready to receive it, or destroyed by those who would harm it. When Salih says the guardians of Allah have kept themselves hidden &quot;by <span class="xpl-ar" dir="rtl" lang="ar">تقيّة</span>,&quot; he is not describing fear for its own sake. He is describing the way a lamp is shielded from the wind, or a seed is covered with earth before it can grow.</p><p>The reason the guardian veils himself lies in what happens when he does not. Salih names three kinds of people who stand between the seeker and the guardian:</p><ul><li><p>the striker set in power, who hunts and is never satisfied even when he wins;</p></li><li><p>the gloating enemy, well pleased with himself, always inciting others against the guardian;</p></li><li><p>and the one who loves the guardian yet cannot keep a secret, and so forsakes him in the end.</p></li></ul><p>Even <strong>love is not enough</strong>. A follower who means well but cannot guard what he knows can betray the guardian without intending to — a whispered name, a careless word, and the hunt begins. The concealment protects the seeker from his own weakness as much as it protects the guardian.</p><p>Deepest of all is what concealment preserves. Allah''s proof to His creation must never be cut off; there must always be a just one on the earth by whom people are guided. If the guardian appeared openly to a people not ready to protect him, and was killed, that living link between heaven and earth would be severed. Concealment keeps the door shut against the corrupters while it stays open to the true seeker.</p><p>This is why the guardian is like Moses, who left the city fearful and watchful and did not return to the killing he had escaped. The earth is never empty of the just one of Allah — sometimes <strong>present and manifest, sometimes veiled away in fear</strong> — and the veil itself is a mercy, waiting for hearts sound enough to draw the truth gently out of hiding.</p>', '["تقيّة: from the root و-ق-ى, meaning to guard, shield, or protect. The same root gives تقوى, the God-consciousness that shields a person from harm, and وقاية, a covering. So تقيّة is literally a \"guarding\" — the truth and its bearer wrapped in protection until it is safe to reveal them.", "وليّ: from the root و-ل-ي, meaning nearness and turning toward. From it come \"guardian,\" \"friend,\" and \"one who takes charge.\" The وليّ of Allah is the one drawn near to Him and set over His affair on earth — near enough to convey His command, and needing protection precisely because of that nearness."]');
